<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esen Buqa II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1429–<a href="/years/1462">1462</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esen Buqa II was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> from about 1429 until his death in 1462. He ruled the eastern part of the khanate while his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, contested the throne in the west. He gave refuge to the Kazakh sultans Kerei and Janibek, who broke away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> with their followers in the 1450s and founded the Kazakh Khanate in the Chu and Talas valleys.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esen-buqa-ii.jpg" alt="Esen Buqa II" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moghulistan.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the rival line of Urus Khan, Kerei and his kinsman Janibek, broke with him, and around 1465, the date given by the historian Muhammad Haidar Dughlat, they led their followers south-east into Moghulistan, where <b>Esen Buqa Khan</b> allowed them to settle in the western Chu and Talas valleys.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Divided by civil war, Moghulistan split in the 1460s between Yunus Khan in the west and his brother <b>Esen Buqa</b>'s line in the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a></li></ul></section>
